      "answer": "The context does not contain enough information to trace a complete regulatory path from the FAA to the maintenance crew servicing the CFM LEAP-1B engine.\n\nWhat the context does establish:\n\n- **FAA \u2192 ADs:** 14 CFR Part 39 establishes requirements for correcting unsafe conditions in aircraft, engines, propellers, and appliances (\u00a739.1), and FAA airworthiness directives are legally enforceable rules applying to those products (\u00a739.3).\n- **ADs \u2192 737 MAX:** FAA AD 2024-01-02 requires MCAS Software v2.0 installation within 90 days of its February 15, 2024 effective date, plus FCOM revision, AOA sensor inspection, and compliance reporting.\n- **ADs \u2192 engine mount maintenance:** The 737 MAX CMM (rev12) lists FAA AD 2024-01-02 and AD 2022-03-07 as related ADs for LEAP-1B engine mount inspection, and its revision history states rev12 updated inspection intervals per AD 2024-01-02 compliance.\n- **Operator-level compliance:** The G-ABCD compliance report shows an operator tracking AD 2024-01-02 against MCAS v1.7.3 with a May 15, 2024 deadline, and the inspection report shows AD compliance verified during a C-Check.\n\nHowever, the context does not describe an explicit chain linking FAA issuance through the CMM to the maintenance crew's servicing actions for the LEAP-1B engine mount, nor does it state that the CMM revision was mandated by the AD. Those connections would be inference beyond the evidence.",
